      "text": "Pseudo-Label",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Pseudo-Label",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "DhG236_2MWYE3Zuv8kzg5",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1006.0678949526376,
      "y": 1163.6128358840942,
      "width": 934.6541748046875,
      "height": 950,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "VzwX1Ft_YdCTCgwn885oK"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "au",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1342235860,
      "version": 805,
      "versionNonce": 1335460460,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789247610584,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Neural networks often need large amounts of labeled data for training, but labelling data can\nbe time-consuming and costly. In contrast, unlabeled data is usually much easier and cheaper to\ncollect. \n\nTo overcome this challenge, semi-supervised learning has become an important approach in deep\nlearning. Semi-supervised methods can train more accurate and efficient models by combining a\nsmall amount of unlabeled data.\n\nIn general, there are two main ways to use unlabeled data: pretraining and generating pseudo\nlabels. The main idea of pseudo-labelling is to first train a model on labelled data, use it to\ncreate pseudo-labels for the unlabeled data, and then train a new model to learn from these\npseudo-labels.\n\nThe idea is: the teacher first generates a transcription, and the student is trained to\nreproduce that transcription token by token.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe paper's displayed equation uses P directly, but conceptually it is functioning as the\npseudo-label cross-entropy objective so we want to minimize negative log-likelihood.\n\n\n\n\n\nThis loss measures the difference between two probability distributions; the one generated\nby the language model head and the real distribution. The true distribution of tokens is\nrepresented by a one-hot vector, where the position of the 1 correspondes to the vocabulary\nID of the token to be predicted.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Neural networks often need large amounts of labeled data for training, but labelling data can be time-consuming and costly. In contrast, unlabeled data is usually much easier and cheaper to collect. \n\nTo overcome this challenge, semi-supervised learning has become an important approach in deep learning. Semi-supervised methods can train more accurate and efficient models by combining a small amount of unlabeled data.\n\nIn general, there are two main ways to use unlabeled data: pretraining and generating pseudo labels. The main idea of pseudo-labelling is to first train a model on labelled data, use it to create pseudo-labels for the unlabeled data, and then train a new model to learn from these pseudo-labels.\n\nThe idea is: the teacher first generates a transcription, and the student is trained to reproduce that transcription token by token.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe paper's displayed equation uses P directly, but conceptually it is functioning as the pseudo-label cross-entropy objective so we want to minimize negative log-likelihood.\n\n\n\n\n\nThis loss measures the difference between two probability distributions; the one generated\nby the language model head and the real distribution. The true distribution of tokens is\nrepresented by a one-hot vector, where the position of the 1 correspondes to the vocabulary\nID of the token to be predicted.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
